<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zealots</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zealots were a Jewish political and religious movement of the 1st century CE that sought to drive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> a Zealot faction led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a> seized the Temple in 68, killed moderate leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananus-ben-ananus">Ananus ben Ananus</a> and fought rival rebel groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The name later came to mean any fanatical partisan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word zealot translates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> kanai, meaning one who is zealous on behalf of God, and derives from the Greek zēlōtēs, an emulator or zealous admirer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> used zealotry for a fourth sect or fourth Jewish philosophy beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a>. He says it was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee">Judas of Galilee</a> in 6 CE against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius">Quirinius</a>, and that its followers agreed with the Pharisees in other things but had an inviolable attachment to liberty and held that God was their only Ruler and Lord. Scholars note that the sources never clearly identify this fourth philosophy with the Zealots, and some argue the group was less clearly marked out before the war of 66 to 70 or 73.</p><p>At the core of zealotry was a total commitment to God's will and law, modelled on Phinehas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> and the Hasmonean priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a>. It was also driven by a belief in Israel's election by God.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Zealots took the lead in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, which lasted from 66 to 73 CE, objecting to Roman rule and seeking to remove it by force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, whose account is hostile, describes a murderous reign of terror in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> before the destruction of the Temple and says the Zealots followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gischala">John of Gischala</a>. The group around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a> is the only one known to have explicitly adopted the title. They held Jerusalem until 70, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a>, the son of Emperor Vespasian, retook the city and destroyed Herod's Temple.</p><p>The Sicarii, who carried small daggers called sicae under their cloaks, were a distinct and rival faction with similar principles. Their leader early in the revolt was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menahem">Menahem</a> ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee">Judas of Galilee</a>. According to the historian Jonathan Price, the murder of Menahem and the expulsion of many of his followers in 66 was part of a struggle for control of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> calls the Zealots the Biryonim, meaning ruffians, and blames them for the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, citing their destruction of the city's stores of food and firewood during the siege. In the early 70s, Sicarii activists tried to raise rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> but were put down. Philip Alexander writes that the persistence of Zealot ideas prepared the way for the diaspora uprisings of 115 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt of 132. Simon the Zealot appears among the apostles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zealots” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Jewish–Roman wars, in which 20,000 Idumaeans joined the <b>Zealots</b> at the siege of Jerusalem in 70 CE according to Josephus, the Idumaean people disappear from written history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its moderate priestly leadership was overthrown in 68 by <b>Zealots</b> allied with Idumaeans, who killed Ananus.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-judea">Provisional Government of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
